<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Evaluation setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span> 
For downstream fine-tuning, we use three representative tasks: SST-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Socher et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, AGNEWS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
For each downstream task, we fine-tune the model using <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> fine-tuning samples.
In addition, we mix a proportion <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of unsafe data sampled from AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> into the fine-tuning corpus to simulate harmful fine-tuning scenarios.
Following the experimental settings used in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> (4500 downstream task data and 500 unsafe data) for our main results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> 
To evaluate the effectiveness of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span>, we use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team and others, 2024</a>)</cite>, Gemma-2-9b-it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Llama-3.1-8b-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Llama-3.2-1b-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We first conduct a preliminary evaluation of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct to assess the effectiveness of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> across three datasets.
We then scale the model size as Llama-3.1-8b-Instruct, Gemma-2-9b-it, and Llama-3.2-1b-Instruct to demonstrate that our approach can be applied to larger models, different model families and remains robust as model capacity increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> 
Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, we evaluate models using fine-tuning accuracy ACC (The top-1 accuracy on the test sets of downstream tasks) and attack successful rate(ASR), which measure downstream task performance and adherence to safety alignment under harmful prompts, respectively.
To ensure a reliable and comprehensive evaluation of safety behavior, we measure safety rates across multiple widely used benchmarks, including StrongREJECT (SR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, JailbreakBench (JB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and HarmBench (HB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
For all safety evaluations, we employ <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-Guard-3-8B</span> as the judge model to determine the safety of the model’s response and calculate the harmful score ASR.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> 
We compare <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> with 6 baselines: Initial model, vanilla supervised fine-tuning (SFT), SafeLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Constrained SFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and AsFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We employ LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> for efficient fine-tuning of all the models, with a rank of 8 across all experiments. The AdamW optimizer is used with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\text{\times}{10}^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\text{\times}{10}^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, training for 3 epochs with a batch size of 2. The coefficient of our PACT regularization term <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 3.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> across three dimensions: generalization to different fine-tuning datasets, performance across model architectures and sizes, and robustness to varying proportions of harmful data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization across fine-tuning datasets.</span> 
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4. Evaluations ‣ Few Tokens, Big Leverage: Preserving Safety Alignment by Constraining Safety Tokens during Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the results for Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct fine-tuned on three diverse tasks, each with 10% harmful data.
We can see that vanilla SFT consistently achieves high task accuracy but suffers significant safety degradation across all datasets.
For example, the ASRs on the HB dataset are consistently higher than 94% across all models.
This confirms that even a small proportion of harmful data (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}500</annotation></semantics></math>) can substantially compromise model safety.
In such cases, the models lose the direct refusal ability completely.
Existing defense methods exhibit various trade-offs.
SafeLora preserves some safety on GSM8K, but fails completely on SST2 and AGNEWS.
Constrained SFT maintains safety on all the datasets, but they substantially degrade task performance on GSM8K.
AsFT provides a better utility–safety trade-off than Constrained-SFT and Safe LoRA, but still shows elevated jailbreak and harmful behavior rates, particularly on SST2.
In contrast, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> consistently achieves the lowest ASRs across all three datasets while maintaining comparable task accuracy.
For example, we attain 80.89% accuracy on GSM8K while reducing ASR on HB to 29.50%.
With only marginal accuracy trade-offs, we can approach almost the same safety level of the initial model.
These results demonstrate effective generalization across diverse task domains (mathematical reasoning, sentiment classification, and topic classification) under data poisoning scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization across model architectures..</span> 
To assess generalization across model architectures and scales, we further evaluate on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, and Gemma-2-9B-it, all fine-tuned on GSM8K with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> harmful data.
The results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4. Evaluations ‣ Few Tokens, Big Leverage: Preserving Safety Alignment by Constraining Safety Tokens during Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
We can see that existing baselines exhibit inconsistent behavior across models.
SafeLoRA preserves safety on Llama-3.1-8B but fails on Gemma-2-9B, while AsFT achieves low risk on Llama-3.2-1B but catastrophically degrades on Llama-3.1-8B.
Such inconsistency indicates that these defenses are sensitive to model architectures and sizes.
In contrast, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> consistently achieves the best or near-best safety metrics across all 3 models without sacrificing utility.
Notably, for model Llama-3.1-8B, ASR on HB is reduced to 0.00%, while accuracy remains comparable to SFT.
On Gemma-2-9B, most baselines suffer substantial safety degradation, but <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> constraints the ASR on HB at 14.50% and achieves the highest task accuracy.
These results confirm that our method generalizes reliably across diverse model families and scales without architecture-specific tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness to harmful data proportions..</span> 
Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4. Evaluations ‣ Few Tokens, Big Leverage: Preserving Safety Alignment by Constraining Safety Tokens during Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the performance under varying poison ratios on the AGNEWS dataset.
We can see that the baseline methods exhibit sharp safety degradation as the poison ratio increases.
For example, SafeLoRA maintains safety at <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=0\%</annotation></semantics></math> but collapses at <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=5\%</annotation></semantics></math> (SR: 71.57%, HB: 93.50%).
AsFT still exhibits substantial risk at higher poison ratios (HB: 70.50% at <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=10\%</annotation></semantics></math>).
While <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> consistently achieves the lowest safety risk across all poison ratios while maintaining task accuracy.
Notably, even in the benign setting (<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=0\%</annotation></semantics></math>), <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> slightly improves upon the initial model’s safety (SR: 0.96% vs. 1.60%).
It shows that <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> can mitigate latent safety risks introduced by the fine-tuning process itself, independent of explicit harmful data.
As the poison ratio increases to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PACT</span> remains stable, unlike the other existing approaches.</p>
</div>
